<!-- WHY VISIT -->
<section class="section"><div class="container">
  <div class="section-header reveal"><h2>Why Visit Mongolia</h2><div class="divider"></div></div>
  <div class="why-grid">
    <div class="why-card reveal"><div class="why-icon">&#127965;</div><h3>Gobi Desert</h3><p>Stand atop towering sand dunes at Khongoryn Els — "Singing Sands" that hum as the wind sweeps across them. Unearth dinosaur fossils at the Flaming Cliffs of Bayanzag, where the world's first dinosaur eggs were discovered. The Gobi is a land of extremes — burning red sunsets, snow-dusted mountains, and endless horizons.</p></div>
    <div class="why-card reveal"><div class="why-icon">&#128014;</div><h3>Nomadic Culture</h3><p>Stay in a traditional ger (yurt) camp and live as Mongolian nomads have for centuries. Ride sturdy Mongol horses across open steppes, learn to prepare traditional dairy products, and listen to khoomii throat singing under a canopy of a billion stars. The nomads' legendary hospitality is life-changing.</p></div>
    <div class="why-card reveal"><div class="why-icon">&#127747;</div><h3>Ulaanbaatar</h3><p>Explore the world's coldest capital city, where Soviet-era architecture meets Buddhist monasteries and modern art galleries. The Gandantegchinlen Monastery, bustling Narantuul Black Market, and the newly revitalized central district offer a fascinating window into Mongolia's rapid transformation.</p></div>
    <div class="why-card reveal"><div class="why-icon">&#127757;</div><h3>Vast Landscapes</h3><p>Mongolia is one of the least densely populated countries on Earth — with barely two people per square kilometer. Drive for hours across rolling steppes, alpine forests, towering sand dunes, and volcanic valleys without seeing another soul. This is the ultimate escape into raw, untamed nature.</p></div>
  </div>
</div></section>

<!-- TOP ATTRACTIONS -->
<section class="section section-alt"><div class="container">
  <div class="section-header reveal"><h2>Top Attractions</h2><div class="divider"></div></div>
  <div class="tours-grid">
    <div class="tour-card reveal">
      <div class="tour-image" style="position:relative;"><img loading="lazy" src="/assets/images/hero-silkroad.jpg" alt="Gobi Desert and Khongoryn Els"><div class="tour-badge">#1</div></div>
      <div class="tour-body"><h3>Gobi Desert &amp; Khongoryn Els</h3><p>The Gobi is Mongolia's crown jewel — a vast desert spanning 1.3 million square kilometers. Walk the magnificent Khongoryn Els sand dunes stretching 180 km, some reaching 300 meters high. Visit Bayanzag's Flaming Cliffs glowing crimson at sunset, where you can still find dinosaur bone fragments scattered across the ground.</p></div>
    </div>
    <div class="tour-card reveal">
      <div class="tour-image" style="position:relative;"><img loading="lazy" src="/assets/images/hero-silkroad.jpg" alt="Terelj National Park"><div class="tour-badge">#2</div></div>
      <div class="tour-body"><h3>Terelj National Park</h3><p>Just 70 km from Ulaanbaatar, Terelj offers dramatic granite rock formations, wildflower-covered alpine meadows, and the iconic Turtle Rock. Stay in a riverside ger camp, hike to the Aryabal Meditation Temple perched on a hillside, and ride horses through pine-scented valleys. The perfect introduction to Mongolia's landscapes.</p></div>
    </div>
    <div class="tour-card reveal">
      <div class="tour-image" style="position:relative;"><img loading="lazy" src="/assets/images/hero-silkroad.jpg" alt="Karakorum"><div class="tour-badge">#3</div></div>
      <div class="tour-body"><h3>Karakorum &mdash; Ancient Mongol Capital</h3><p>Walk the ground where Genghis Khan established his empire's capital in 1220. Today the Erdene Zuu Monastery — Mongolia's oldest surviving Buddhist monastery — rises from the ruins with 108 white stupas and three gleaming temples. The nearby Karakorum Museum houses stunning artifacts from the Silk Road era.</p></div>
    </div>
    <div class="tour-card reveal">
      <div class="tour-image" style="position:relative;"><img loading="lazy" src="/assets/images/hero-silkroad.jpg" alt="Lake Khovsgol"><div class="tour-badge">#4</div></div>
      <div class="tour-body"><h3>Lake Khovsgol</h3><p>Known as the "Blue Pearl of Mongolia," Lake Khovsgol holds nearly 70% of Mongolia's fresh water and is one of the world's most pristine ancient lakes. Surrounded by taiga forests and snow-capped mountains, the turquoise waters are perfect for kayaking, horseback trekking, and meeting the Tsaatan reindeer herders who live nearby.</p></div>
    </div>
    <div class="tour-card reveal">
      <div class="tour-image" style="position:relative;"><img loading="lazy" src="/assets/images/hero-silkroad.jpg" alt="Orkhon Valley"><div class="tour-badge">#5</div></div>
      <div class="tour-body"><h3>Orkhon Valley</h3><p>A UNESCO World Heritage site, the Orkhon Valley was the cradle of nomadic civilization for over two millennia. Ride on horseback past ancient Turkic stone monuments (balbals), the ruins of the Uighur capital Khar Balgas, and the stunning Orkhon Waterfall. Nomadic families still graze their herds here as they have for centuries.</p></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div></section>

<!-- BEST TIME TO VISIT -->
<section class="section"><div class="container">
  <div class="section-header reveal"><h2>Best Time to Visit</h2><div class="divider"></div></div>
  <div class="why-grid">
    <div class="why-card reveal"><div class="why-icon">&#127774;</div><h3>Summer (Jun-Aug)</h3><p>The ideal season — warm days (20-30&deg;C), lush green steppes, and the famous Naadam Festival in July featuring wrestling, horse racing, and archery. All roads and ger camps are open. Long daylight hours (sun sets after 9 PM) give you maximum time for exploration. Book well in advance — this is peak season.</p></div>
    <div class="why-card reveal"><div class="why-icon">&#127800;</div><h3>Spring (Mar-May)</h3><p>A shoulder season with rapidly changing weather — temperatures range from 5-20&deg;C. The steppe turns green in May and baby animals appear. Fewer tourists mean lower prices and more authentic experiences. Be prepared for occasional sandstorms and sudden temperature drops — layers are essential.</p></div>
    <div class="why-card reveal"><div class="why-icon">&#127810;</div><h3>Autumn (Sep-Oct)</h3><p>Golden larch forests, crisp air, and spectacular photography conditions. Temperatures fall rapidly from 15&deg;C in September to near freezing by late October. The tourist crowds thin out dramatically after August. September's Eagle Festival in western Mongolia is a highlight for photographers and cultural enthusiasts.</p></div>
    <div class="why-card reveal"><div class="why-icon">&#10052;</div><h3>Winter (Nov-Feb)</h3><p>Extreme cold — temperatures can plunge to -40&deg;C. Only for the truly adventurous. Ulaanbaatar is the world's coldest national capital. Few tourist facilities operate outside the city, but winter offers stunning frozen landscapes, ice festivals, and the chance to experience Mongolia at its most raw. Serious cold-weather gear required.</p></div>
  </div>
</div></section>

